In addition to supporting them when tapping on the icons, the code will also need to be updated to play them when the question/answer is presented. The sounds available on the front and back of the card can be retrieved via card.questionAvTags() and card.answerAvTags(). This includes sound tags as well. The legacy path currently has its own logic for gathering sound tags which is rather different, so it may make sense to defer this work until the backend migration is nearly complete, so the old code can be culled at the same time.

Hello all. When using the `<tts>` tag to only read a small selection of text, cloze `[...]` are not read as "blank." TTS simply doesn't read them.

Perhaps the `.text().replace(TemplateFilters.CLOZE_DELETION_REPLACEMENT, clozeReplacement!!)` should be applied outside of the `if` statement below?

```java
object TtsParser {
    /**
     * Returns the list of text snippets contained in the given HTML fragment that should be read
     * using the Android text-to-speech engine, together with the languages they are in.
     *
     *
     * Each returned LocalisedText object contains the text extracted from a &lt;tts&gt; element
     * whose 'service' attribute is set to 'android', and the localeCode taken from the 'voice'
     * attribute of that element. This holds unless the HTML fragment contains no such &lt;tts&gt;
     * elements; in that case the function returns a single LocalisedText object containing the
     * text extracted from the whole HTML fragment, with the localeCode set to an empty string.
     */
    @JvmStatic
    fun getTextsToRead(html: String, clozeReplacement: String?): List<TTSTag> {
        val textsToRead: MutableList<TTSTag> = ArrayList()
        val elem = Jsoup.parseBodyFragment(html).body()
        parseTtsElements(elem, textsToRead)
        if (textsToRead.isEmpty()) {
            // No <tts service="android"> elements found: return the text of the whole HTML fragment
            textsToRead.add(readWholeCard(elem.text().replace(TemplateFilters.CLOZE_DELETION_REPLACEMENT, clozeReplacement!!)))
        }
        return textsToRead
    }
```

_Originally posted by @KendallPark in [URL]

Hi guys, for some reason on my Android 12 phone the `AddContentApi.getAnkiDroidPackageName(Context)` returns null, causing Aedict to errorneously report that AnkiDroid is not available.

###### Reproduction Steps

1. Install Aedict
2. Search for `haha` and open the entry details
3. In the upper-right overflow menu there should be a menu item "Export to `AnkiDroid"`

The menu item is hidden since `AddContentApi.getAnkiDroidPackageName(activity);` returns null. Full code of the Anki presence check function:

```java
    private boolean isAnkiDroidInstantAPIAvailable() {
        PackageManager manager = activity.getApplicationContext().getPackageManager();
        try {
            final String ankiDroidPackageName = AddContentApi.getAnkiDroidPackageName(activity);
            if (ankiDroidPackageName == null) {
                return false;
            }
            final ApplicationInfo appInfo = manager.getApplicationInfo(ankiDroidPackageName, 0);
            return true;
        } catch (PackageManager.NameNotFoundException e) {
            return false;
        }
    }
```

###### Expected Result

The `AddContentApi.getAnkiDroidPackageName(activity);` function should not return null

###### Actual Result

`AddContentApi.getAnkiDroidPackageName(activity);` returns null.

###### Debug info
Refer to the [support page]([URL] if you are unsure where to get the "debug info".

* Aedict 3.50.24
* AnkiDroid 2.15.6
* Instant API version: `com.ichi2.anki:api:1.1.0alpha5`

###### 
*Enter an [x] character to confirm the points below:*

- [x] I have read the [support page]([URL] and am reporting a bug or enhancement request specific to AnkiDroid
- [x] I have checked the [manual]([URL] and the [FAQ]([URL] and could not find a solution to my issue
- [x] I have searched for similar existing issues here and on the user forum
- [ ]  ([instructions]([URL]

I'll attach the debug info from AnkiDroid immediately.

The users reported that the same issue persists on Android 11 and 13, and that the ability to export to AnkiDroid was lost a couple of days ago.

Could it be that Google hardened Android and now some additional permissions are required to expose/access Content Providers? I have recently bumped target SDK API to 30 in Aedict, but the page at [URL] says nothing about content providers :thinking: 

Aedict's AndroidManifest.xml lists `<uses-permission android:name="com.ichi2.anki.permission.READ_WRITE_DATABASE"/>` so the access should be granted. Or was it perhaps changed in newer AnkiDroids please?

**Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.**
AnkiDroid provides option to add custom fonts. The fonts file added to `AnkiDroid/fonts` dir and using `Settings -> Appearance -> Fonts` preference, the fonts can be set to use in reviewer, editor and browser. But only one custom font can be used in reviewer so there should be option to multi select fonts that can be used in reviewer. 

**Describe the solution you'd like**
Reviewer option in `Settings -> Appearance -> Fonts` and add multiple fonts.

**Describe alternatives you've considered**
Using css the fonts can be added but when fonts size large there is [flash of invisible text]([URL] and [flash of unstyled text]([URL]

```
@font-face {
    font-family: 'Some-font';
    src: url('Some-font.ttf') format('ttf'),
        url('Some-font.woff2') format('woff2'),
        url('Some-font.woff') format('woff');
    font-weight: 500;
    font-style: normal;
    font-display: swap; 
}
```

Currently ACRA initializes with user language correctly on startup, but if the user switches language you have to restart the app before ACRA's toast and dialog switch languages

Here is the FIXME I left:

```
        // FIXME ACRA needs to reinitialize after language is changed, but with the new language
        //   this is difficult because the Application (AnkiDroidApp) does not change it's baseContext
        //   perhaps a solution could be to change AnkiDroidApp to have a context wrapper that it sets
        //   as baseContext, and that wrapper allows a resources/configuration update, then
        //   in GeneralSettingsFragment for the language dialog change listener, the context wrapper
        //   could be updated directly with the new locale code so that calling getString on would fetch
        //   the new language string ?
```

Here is the related comment:

> I was unable to quickly fix this, the Application context is never updated with the system language, the only way Activities update is that they recreate themselves. I left a big comment instead that we should peel to another issue, with a maybe-useful solution sketch of having the base contexts be wrappers that allow resources/configuration updates post-hoc on language change events vs having them be frozen post-attachBaseContext

I'll have to peel it to another issue in order to get this update in so the PR doesn't decay any more

_Originally posted by @mikehardy in [URL]

I'm learning french and trying to make word cards for French words. And there are genders such as masculine or feminine in french. So I add triggles to let users to choose the gender using html and js (I never learn html or js actually). It works ok on desktop but with some bugs on ankidroid. 

###### Reproduction Steps
1. Install ANKI (windows) and ANKIDROID (in play store, version 2.15.6)
2. Using listed html code to create the template.  
3. Delete all the fields and create fields 'GB', 'FR', 'GENDER' (all letters are in the upper case) before you set your card template
4. Sync. 
5. Try it on ANKI windows and ANKIDROID
(***Setting -> Advanced -> enable html/js debugging does not help*** )
```html
// front side

{{GB}}
<hr>
<border id="genderBorder">
	<input type="radio" name="genderRadio" 
    	onClick = "setGender('m')">Masculin</input>
	<input type="radio" name="genderRadio" 
    	onClick = "setGender('f')">Féminin</input>
	<input type="radio" name="genderRadio" 
    	onClick = "setGender('mf')">Masculin ou Féminin</input>
</border>
{{type:FR}}
<script>
  window.ankiTemplateTmpData = ''; 
  function setGender(g) {
  	window.ankiTemplateTmpData = g;
  }
</script>

```

```html
// back side

{{GB}}
<hr>
<nobr id="presenter"></nobr>
<hr id=answer hidden>
<p>{{type:FR}}</p>

<script>
	var red = '#ffaaaa';
	var green = '#aaffaa';
	var tg = '{{GENDER}}';
    var g = window.ankiTemplateTmpData;
	var isCorrect = 
		g == '' ? '?' : 
		g == tg ? '√' : '×';
    var p = document.getElementById('presenter');
	p.innerHTML= 
		isCorrect 
		+ '&nbsp&nbsp&nbsp&nbsp' 
		+ matchGender('{{GENDER}}');
	if (isCorrect == '√') {
		p.style.backgroundColor = green;
	} else if (isCorrect == '×') {
		p.style.backgroundColor = red;
	} else {
		p.style.backgroundColor = "lightyellow";
	}
	// match m, f, or mf
    function matchGender(g) {
        switch (g) {
        	case 'm':
            	return 'Masculin';
            	break;
    		case 'f':
				return 'Féminin';
				break;
			case 'mf':
				return 'Masculin ou féminin';
				break;
        	default:
            	break;
    	}
    }
</script>
```

###### Expected Result
[SCREENSHOT]
[SCREENSHOT]


###### Actual Result
If we are in the dark mode in ANKIDROID, then we cannot have the result report of "gender". 
If we are in the light mode, gender is correct, but the result will always be "×", and color is always red. 


